Step 1 — Show the Input
"Let me start by showing the input to this simulation. We have a network of four nodes — A, B, C, and D. Each link is defined with two values: capacity, the maximum traffic it can handle, and weight, the routing cost. On top of that, we have a demand matrix — this tells us who wants to send traffic to whom, and how much."

Step 2 — Show the Output
"Now let's look at the output. After running the simulation, we get a table showing each link's usage, capacity, and utilization. The table is sorted from highest to lowest utilization, so the most stressed links appear at the top. For example, B→D shows a utilization of 1.4 — meaning it is carrying 40% more traffic than it was designed to handle."

Step 3 — Explain "Critical Scenario"
"Now, what exactly is a critical scenario?"
"In the double-link failure analysis, we test every possible combination of two links failing at the same time. Among all those combinations, we flag a scenario as critical if either of two conditions is met."
"First — if a demand becomes unreachable. This means there is simply no path left in the network to deliver that traffic. In a real network, this would be a complete service outage for that flow."
"Second — if the maximum utilization of any remaining link exceeds 100%. This means the network is still connected, but at least one link is overloaded beyond its capacity — leading to packet loss and degraded performance."
"So a critical scenario is essentially any failure combination that either breaks connectivity or causes overload. These are the cases a network engineer must design against."

