We define the network topology. We created a directed graph using NetworkX, and add links with two attributes each - capacity and weight. Capacity represents the maximum bandwidth the link can carry, and weight is the routing cost used by the shortest path algorithm. We also define the demand matrix, which specifies who sends how much traffic to whom.

This section contains the core routing logic. The route_demands function iterates over each demand and finds the shortest path using Dijkstra's algorithm. In default mode, all traffic follows a single shortest path. In ECMP mode, if multiple equal-cost paths exist, the traffic is split evenly across all of them. As each demand is routed, the usage value on every link along the path is incremented accordingly.

The compute_utilization function calculates the utilization of each link by dividing its current usage by its capacity. A value of 1.0 means the link is at full capacity, and anything above 1.0 means it is over capacity. The results are sorted in descending order, so the most congested links appear at the top

The draw_graph function visualizes the network using matplotlib. Each node is drawn with its label, and each link is annotated with its current usage over capacity. This makes it easy to visually identify congested links at a glance.

Here the simulation is operated with original weights. The results show that the B->D link has a utilization of 1.4, which means it is already over capacity in the baseline state. This is because all traffic flows converge onto the path through B, overloading that single link.

To reduce congestion, we increase the weight of B->D from 2 to 5. This makes the path through C more cost-effective, so the routing algorithm redirects some traffic onto the C-<D link. After this adjustment, the maximum utilization drops.

This section simulates link failure scenarios. For single-link failures, we remove each link one at a time, re-run the routing, and record the maximum utilization. If a demand becomes unreachable due to the failure, it is flagged separately. The worst-case failure link is defined as the one whose removal results in the highest maximum utilization across the remaining network. For double-link failures, we use itertools.combinations to test every possible pair of failed links,  and identify critical scenarios (cases where demand becomes unreachable or utilization exceeds 100%)

Finally, we print a summary of all results - the baseline maximum utilization, the number of single and double failure scenarios tested, how many caused unreachable demands, and how many were classified as critical. 


